---
title: Proposition 9
---
order: 9
---
short_label: XI.9
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: Straight lines which are parallel to the same straight line but do not lie in the same plane with it are also parallel to each other.
----
proof:

Let each of the straight lines *AB* and *CD* be parallel to *EF,* but not in the same plane with it.

I say that *AB* is parallel to *CD.*

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="270" height="200" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="XI.9 diagram" src="propXI9.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "XI.9",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;220,40",
            "B;point;free;90,40",
            "AB;line;connect;A,B",
            "C;point;free;210,160",
            "D';point;parallelogram;B,A,C;0;0",
            "D;point;lineSlider;C,D',80,160",
            "CD;line;connect;C,D",
            "E;point;free;160,120",
            "F';point;parallelogram;B,A,E;0;0",
            "F;point;lineSlider;E,F',30,120",
            "EF;line;connect;E,F",
            "G;point;lineSlider;EF,100,160",
            "H;point;parallelogram;A,E,G",
            "K;point;parallelogram;C,E,G",
            "GH;line;connect;G,H",
            "GK;line;connect;G,K"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

Let a point *G* be taken at random on *EF,* and from it draw *GH* in the plane through *EF* and *AB* at right angles to *EF,* and *GK* in the plane through *EF* and *CD* again at right angles to *EF.* [!just I.11]

Now, since *EF* is at right angles to each of the straight lines *GH* and *GK,* therefore *EF* is also at right angles to the plane through *GH* and *GK.* [!just XI.4]

And *EF* is parallel to *AB,* therefore *AB* is also at right angles to the plane through *HG* and *GK.* [!just XI.8]

For the same reason *CD* is also at right angles to the plane through *HG* and *GK.* Therefore each of the straight lines *AB* and *CD* is at right angles to the plane through *HG* and *GK.*

But if two straight lines are at right angles to the same plane, then the straight lines are parallel. Therefore *AB* is parallel to *CD.* [!just XI.6]

Therefore, *straight lines which are parallel to the same straight line but do not lie in the same plane with it are also parallel to each other.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
----
guide:

Note that this proposition is the three-dimensional analogue to proposition @I.30.

#### The Varignon parallelogram of space quadrilaterals

<figure class="diagram rdiagram">
<canvas id="canvas_1" width="270" height="250" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="Varignon parallelogram" src="propXI9a.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_1",
        background: "0,0,100",
        title: "Varignon parallelogram",
        elements: [
            "A;point;free;110,40",
            "B;point;free;45,210",
            "C;point;free;220,130",
            "D;point;free;210,210",
            "ABCD;polygon;quadrilateral;A,B,C,D;0;0;black;0",
            "E;point;midpoint;A,B",
            "F;point;midpoint;B,C",
            "G;point;midpoint;C,D",
            "H;point;midpoint;D,A",
            "EFGH;polygon;quadrilateral;E,F,G,H;0;0;black;random",
            "AC;line;connect;A,C;0;0;gray",
            "BD;line;connect;B,D;0;0;gray"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

Consider a quadrilateral *ABCD* whose four vertices may or may not lie in a plane. Let *E, F, G,* and *H* be the midpoints of the sides *AB, BC, CD,* and *DA,* respectively. Then the quadrilateral *EFGH* lies in a plane and is a parallelogram, called the Varignon parallelogram. Varignon (1654–1722) showed the area of a planar quadrilateral is twice the area of this parallelogram.

The proof that *EFGH* is a parallelogram relies on this proposition XI.9 to show the sides are parallel, since it is readily shown that both *EF* and *HG* are parallel to the line *AC,* and both *FG* and *EH* are parallel to the line *BD.*

As a corollary, it follows that the lines joining the midpoints of an arbitrary quadrilateral are concurrent and bisect each other, even if the four sides of the quadrilateral do not lie in a plane. (These are the lines *EG* and *FH* which are not drawn in the diagram.)

#### Use of this proposition

This proposition is used in the proof of the next proposition as well as others in this and the next book.
